findings.defect:
The new views over the real renderer are the right evidence, but two of the four put the HUD over a still of a different flight state.
- real-ascent puts the HUD's 9 228 m, M 1.17 and 20.8 kPa over budget-ascent-10-40-km.png. That still has a black-navy sky, strong Earth curvature and the cloud deck far below: a picture of well over 20 km.
- real-lunarSurface puts a landed HUD (GEAR lit, 0.0 m/s, throttle 0 %, '0.0 m · on target') over budget-lunar-descent.png, in which the lander is in the air. Its shadow lies about 900 px (4K) to the right of its feet. A detached shadow next to a HUD that says landed reads as a rendering fault.
- real-reentry has a thin red streak across the sky at x 3230-3415, y 885 (4K), beside the stage stack. It is part of the still and should go back to its owner.

40 of the 56 pictures still sit over the lab backdrop:
- a gridded flat sea (pad, ascent, chutes, warp-refused, stage-lockout);
- a faceted grey plane for the Moon (lunarOrbit, lunarSurface, terrain-pending);
- a hard-edged flat planet with stars printed inside the blue atmosphere band (reentry-4k, y 700-960);
- a grey daytime sky full of stars (bright-ascent);
- grey cylinders for the vehicle.

retrograde-hold-720p/-4k load the same URL as chutes. They are pixel-identical apart from the sea animation and show HOLD · STABILITY, so no retrograde hold is ever shown.

After the glass and gunmetal pass, the bottom cluster shows four looks within about 400 px:
- gunmetal SAS and gauge panels;
- the black-glass speed window and chips;
- the machined bezel;
- the MANEUVER panel: a navy plate with a light-blue 1 px border that matches neither material.

On the right, the stage list and resources keep the old slate plate, while the left column is black glass with tab headers. This is polish, but the MANEUVER panel is what the player reads through every burn.
